comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-25T05:06:28.595Z","harness:codex","Duplicate check evidence: inspected all 2543 items and 54141 history events; searched every lifecycle state for 2026-07-28 stateless discover subscriptions MRTR tasks caching headers authorization schema and deprecations; reviewed all 33 existing MCP children. No item owns this protocol revision. Official source: https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28"
  "2026-08-28T20:39:18.520Z","harness:codex","Live standards review confirms MCP 2026-07-28 remains the normative implemented baseline for this programme. The official post-release roadmap is now separated into pm-opw1l3 rather than being misrepresented as part of the released specification: pm-ngofg9 event-driven messaging, pm-mrh0ae HTTP-native transport convergence, pm-gstv4k agent identity and delegation, pm-3g3f8z progressive discovery and canonical tool results, pm-4fwn0o server cards, and pm-pbo502 continuous upstream conformance ingestion. Each item is typed to its existing canonical owners and current-spec evidence."
body: "## Delivery horizons\n\n- Foundation: build an authoritative requirement-to-code-to-test matrix from the official 2026-07-28 schema and changelog.\n- Protocol core: adopt stateless per-request version and capability metadata plus mandatory server discovery and result types.\n- Interaction and streaming: implement multi round-trip requests, subscription listening, progress, cancellation, and extension negotiation.\n- Server and extension surface: complete tools, resources, prompts, tasks, skills, and MCP Apps where pm exposes useful capabilities.\n- Trust boundary: implement transport headers, cache policy, JSON Schema 2020-12 behavior, authorization issuer binding, telemetry propagation, consent, and deprecation migrations.\n- Proof: test official schema conformance, legacy probes, negative cases, real stdio and Streamable HTTP processes, packed installs, npx, bunx, and exact released artifacts.\n\n## Architecture constraint\n\nProtocol semantics and typed envelopes belong to the public SDK. The CLI and MCP process are thin adapters. Compatibility is explicit, versioned, bounded, and never inferred from connection-local state."
